# A floor is checked from BOTH sides, because it decays from the side nobody
# watches.
#
# **What a floor is for.** Every counting suite here holds one: a walk that finds
# nothing reports green, an extractor that reads the wrong shape returns an empty
# list and every comparison under it passes vacuously. So each suite counts what
# it found and refuses to pass when the count is below a number somebody wrote
# down. That half has always worked.
#
# **THE HALF THAT DID NOT, measured 2026-09-12.** A floor is a premise about the
# world — *"there are 144 modules today"* — and `.claude/rules/module-shape.md`
# says exactly what that costs: *a fact about the value cannot expire; a premise
# about the world expires silently, and the comment justifying it goes on reading
# as correct.* The tree grows, the floor does not, and the net it holds goes
# slack with nothing ever turning red. The census that opened M-declared-thresholds
# measured the slack, and it was not small:
#
#     LEAST_WALKS        10 against ~30   +200%
#     LEAST_MARKS        14 against 23    +64%
#     emission          142 against 225   +58%
#     canonical         340 against 521   +53%
#     layout            140 against 202   +44%
#     golden/check       65 against 112   +42%
#
# **`suite_canonical` is the proof rather than the argument.** Its floor was
# written for a defect its own comment records: the first draft used the flat
# walk and found 325 files of 371, and 340 caught it. The tree is 521 now, so the
# same fault — a walk reaching 88% — would find 458 and pass. That floor would no
# longer catch the defect it was written for.
#
# **What this module does about it, and what it deliberately does NOT.** It does
# not keep a register, and no list of floors exists anywhere: a second list is a
# second thing to forget, and the suites already count what they need. It adds
# the other comparison to the one they were already making. A floor that the tree
# has outgrown by more than `SLACK` is reported, with the number to write down,
# so re-basing is a line somebody edits on purpose rather than a thing nobody
# notices.
#
# **The repair is always to RAISE the floor to today's count**, never to delete
# the check and never to lower anything. That is the ratchet, and it is the one
# shape panel 134's historian found that ever held: the number moves one way, and
# the human is left with nothing to decide except when.
#
# **The two sides are not the same kind of finding, and since 2026-10-09 they
# are not the same outcome** (defect 536). A count BELOW its floor is a walk that
# stopped reaching the tree: every comparison under it is about to pass
# vacuously, and it is a failure, as it always was. A count PAST its floor is a
# tree that grew: nothing under it is wrong today, and what decays is the floor's
# reach, slowly, the housekeeping the author named on 2026-09-12. Until defect
# 536 both were a red, and over batches 8 to 15 the high side was gate B's most
# frequent one, about 24 reds of 26, none a defect of the compiler. The
# optimistic chain of 2026-10-09 (`.claude/rules/verification.md`) asked that it
# stop turning a batch red while the decay side stayed. So the high side passes
# and is TOLD: `report.keep_outgrown` keeps the floor with the number to write,
# the verdict prints it beside the skips, and the suite's line counts it
# (`report.counted`), so every run says it and none goes red on it. What it still
# asks is the same raise, made on purpose rather than to unblock a gate.

use report
use strings

# How far a count may run ahead of its floor before the floor is reported as
# outgrown, in percent.
#
# **25 and not a tighter number, because this is housekeeping** (author,
# 2026-09-12: the floors are the contract ceiling's case, tidying rather than a
# forcing function). A quarter is a tree that has grown visibly since anybody
# last looked, and it is wide enough that ordinary work never trips it: adding a
# golden case or a module moves a count by one.
#
# It is also the number that makes the six rows above fire today, which is the
# only way to know the check is not decoration — every one of them is over 25%,
# and four are over 40%.
#
# Since defect 536 (2026-10-09) crossing it is told and counted rather than
# failed, so the number decides when a floor is named, never whether a gate
# passes.
constant SLACK: i64
    25

# The floor, checked both ways.
#
# `found` is what the suite counted, `floor` the number it holds, `what` the
# thing being counted, in the suite's own words, for the message.
# The same check where the caller has to STOP on a bad count; a floor outgrown
# is told and is not a reason to stop.
#
# Most floors guard an extractor, and everything under one reads what the
# extractor produced: a check that carries on after an empty read does not fail
# twice, it passes vacuously on the second comparison. Those callers wrote
# `if found < FLOOR ... return`, so they need the verdict back rather than only
# in the report. `true` means the count is sound and the caller may go on.
function sound(@r: report.Report, label: str, found: i64, floor: i64, what: str) -> bool
    before = r.failed
    check(@r, label: label, found: found, floor: floor, what: what)
    return r.failed == before

function check(@r: report.Report, label: str, found: i64, floor: i64, what: str)
    if found < floor
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: "  the walk found " + found.to_str() + " " + what + " and had " + floor.to_str() + " — it is not reaching the tree, or it is reading the wrong shape, and every comparison under it was about to pass vacuously: a check that stops testing its subject reports the property it has stopped testing"
        )
        return

    # The count is sound whatever the high side says, so it is a pass; a floor
    # the tree has outgrown is told beside it, never failed (defect 536).
    if outgrown(found: found, floor: floor)
        report.keep_outgrown(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: "  " + what + ": " + found.to_str() + " against a floor of " + floor.to_str() + ", which the tree has outgrown by more than " + SLACK.to_str() + "%.\n  RAISE the floor to " + found.to_str() + ", on purpose and in a commit that says so. A floor is a premise about the world and it expires in silence: this one now passes a walk that misses " + (found - floor).to_str() + " of them, which is the defect it was written to catch.\n  Told and counted, never a failure: never lower a count to fit, and never delete the check; the number moves one way, and what is left to decide is when."
        )
    report.keep_pass(@r)

# The comparison itself, written once so a test can reach it without a report.
#
# `found * 100 > floor * (100 + SLACK)` and not a division, because integer
# division truncates and would put the boundary a hair in the wrong place —
# which for a threshold is the difference between firing and not.
function outgrown(found: i64, floor: i64) -> bool
    # **A floor of zero is never outgrown**, and the guard is here rather than at
    # the call sites because the arithmetic says the opposite of what is meant:
    # any count at all is more than 25% above nothing, so every suite holding an
    # unset floor would go red through the side that is supposed to be quiet.
    # The low side still catches the empty walk, which is what a zero floor was
    # failing to do in the first place.
    if floor <= 0
        return false
    return found * 100 > floor * (100 + SLACK)

test "a count under its floor fails, and the message names both numbers"
    r: report.Report @= report.empty()
    check(@r, label: "x", found: 37, floor: 140, what: "modules")
    assert r.failed == 1
    assert r.passed == 0

test "a count that has outgrown its floor passes, and is told with the number to write"
    # `suite_canonical`'s real pair on the day this was written, which is the
    # row that proves the check is not decoration. Until defect 536 it was a
    # failure; it is a pass now, and the floor is told and counted beside it.
    r: report.Report @= report.empty()
    check(@r, label: "x", found: 521, floor: 340, what: "files")
    assert r.failed == 0
    assert r.passed == 1
    assert r.outgrown.len() == 1
    assert strings.starts_with(text: r.outgrown[0], prefix: "FLOOR x\n  files: 521 against a floor of 340")
    assert strings.contains(text: r.outgrown[0], needle: "RAISE the floor to 521")
    assert strings.contains(text: r.outgrown[0], needle: "misses 181 of them")
    assert report.counted(r) == "1 passed, 0 failed, 1 floor outgrown"

    # The caller that stops on a bad count is not stopped by a tree that grew,
    # and is stopped by one that shrank below its floor.
    grown: report.Report @= report.empty()
    assert sound(@grown, label: "x", found: 521, floor: 340, what: "files")
    assert grown.outgrown.len() == 1
    shrunk: report.Report @= report.empty()
    assert !sound(@shrunk, label: "x", found: 300, floor: 340, what: "files")
    assert shrunk.outgrown.len() == 0

test "an ordinary count passes, and so does one exactly at the edge"
    r: report.Report @= report.empty()
    check(@r, label: "x", found: 150, floor: 140, what: "modules")
    assert r.passed == 1
    assert r.failed == 0
    assert r.outgrown.len() == 0

    # The edge through `check` itself, so the told side and the arithmetic
    # below cannot part: 125 against 100 is told nothing, 126 is told.
    edge: report.Report @= report.empty()
    check(@edge, label: "x", found: 125, floor: 100, what: "modules")
    assert edge.outgrown.len() == 0
    check(@edge, label: "x", found: 126, floor: 100, what: "modules")
    assert edge.outgrown.len() == 1
    assert edge.failed == 0

    # The boundary, asserted on both sides, because a threshold tested from one
    # side only is a threshold nobody has tested. 125 is 25% above 100 and
    # passes; 126 does not.
    assert !outgrown(found: 125, floor: 100)
    assert outgrown(found: 126, floor: 100)

    # And equality passes: a count sitting exactly on its floor is a floor doing
    # its job, not one being outgrown.
    assert !outgrown(found: 140, floor: 140)

test "a zero floor is never outgrown, and a zero count fails the low side"
    # A floor of zero is a floor nobody set. It must not turn every suite red
    # through the high side, and the low side still catches an empty walk.
    assert !outgrown(found: 900, floor: 0)
    r: report.Report @= report.empty()
    check(@r, label: "x", found: 0, floor: 1, what: "cases")
    assert r.failed == 1
